**Reused evidence**
I reused only the prior social-protection item that materially fits 6B. I did not reuse the presidency or constitution items because they are attribution context, not welfare evidence.
REUSE_JSON: {"evidence_ids":["E0057"],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}

**New evidence**
The retained 2022 evidence clusters around crisis-response social protection, food security, health-system reform, WASH, education access, and continuing child-poverty and learning gaps.

SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"US$ 500 Million Project Will Help to Strengthen Egypt's Food and Nutrition Security","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-06-29","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/06/28/us-500-million-project-will-help-to-strengthen-egypt-food-and-nutrition-security","claim":"The World Bank approved a US$500 million project to keep poor and vulnerable Egyptian households supplied with bread, cushion Ukraine-war price shocks, and support food-security reforms; it tied the project to Egypt's Bread Subsidy Program and about one month of wheat supply for roughly 70 million low-income Egyptians, including about 31 million people under the poverty line.","locator":"press release paragraphs 1-6, especially lines 143-149","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous World Bank press release with specific project terms, beneficiary counts, and policy rationale.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"exactly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Sisi government as the borrower-side authority and policy sponsor, though the shock context was heavily external.","contrary_evidence":["The project is a response to crisis pressure, not proof of structural welfare improvement by year-end.","The same text emphasizes pandemic and Ukraine-war shocks."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.6","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"New World Bank Financing Will Expand and Strengthen Egypt's Flagship Social Protection Program","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-12-23","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/press-release/2022/12/23/new-world-bank-financing-will-expand-and-strengthen-egypt-s-flagship-social-protection-program","claim":"The World Bank announced US$500 million to expand Egypt's Takaful and Karama program, saying it covered 3.69 million households as of June 2022, that women were 74 percent of card holders/direct beneficiaries, and that 67 percent of the cash went to Upper Egypt.","locator":"lines 146-154","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Primary World Bank press release with direct coverage, targeting, and distribution details.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"exactly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Sisi government as the policy sponsor, though co-designed with the World Bank and focused on implementation through the Ministry of Social Solidarity.","contrary_evidence":["This shows mitigation and targeting, not a full answer to poverty or labor-market weakness.","It does not prove that vulnerable households were adequately protected from inflation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.6","6B.8","6B.9","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Protecting and Fulfilling the Rights of Children In Egypt","publisher":"UNICEF Egypt","publication_date":"2023-01","url":"https://www.unicef.org/egypt/reports/protecting-and-fulfilling-rights-children-egypt","claim":"UNICEF said poverty had risen despite government programmes, nearly 30 percent of Egyptians lived in monetary poverty, children in poorer backgrounds faced a triple burden of malnutrition, and at least 1 million school-age children with disabilities were out of school along with many refugee and migrant children.","locator":"highlights section, lines 65-74","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF country report is a strong local synthesis, but it is retrospective and not a direct audit of Sisi's personal choices in 2022.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"retrospective on the 2022 period and the immediate surrounding years","ruler_attribution":"Country-level welfare context and criticism of government performance; attribution to Sisi is indirect through national policy responsibility.","contrary_evidence":["The same report notes progress in mothers' and children's health and government programmes to expand basic services.","It is a retrospective synthesis, not a year-end implementation audit."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Recovering from COVID-19 Learning Losses","publisher":"UNICEF Egypt","publication_date":"2022-06-23","url":"https://www.unicef.org/egypt/stories/recovering-covid-19-learning-losses","claim":"UNICEF said marginalized children, especially migrant and refugee children, were below expected learning levels in literacy and numeracy after COVID-19, and that UNICEF was working with Egypt's education ministry and partners to reach close to 30,000 children with a remediation programme and assessment tools.","locator":"story text, lines 67-69 and 96-103","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous UNICEF country story with explicit Egypt-specific remedial action and target group descriptions.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"exactly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the national education system under the Sisi government, but the remedial action was jointly implemented with UNICEF and external donors.","contrary_evidence":["The story emphasizes learning loss and unequal access rather than a completed recovery.","It is a program description, not a final outcome evaluation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.5","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt (EGY) - Demographics, Health & Infant Mortality","publisher":"UNICEF DATA","publication_date":"best available; page shows 2022 indicators","url":"https://data.unicef.org/country/egy/","claim":"UNICEF's Egypt country dashboard reports 2022 primary-school attendance at 97 percent and primary completion at 91 percent, with lower-secondary attendance at 85 percent and upper-secondary attendance at 70 percent.","locator":"education section, lines 5438-5474","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF country dashboard is a primary indicator source; the values are directly visible on the page.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"2022 indicator snapshot","ruler_attribution":"Country-level outcome context only; not a direct ruler-action measure.","contrary_evidence":["High attendance does not eliminate quality or completion gaps for poorer and marginalized children.","The page does not isolate Sisi's personal contribution."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Egypt (EGY) - Demographics, Health & Infant Mortality","publisher":"UNICEF DATA","publication_date":"best available; page shows 2022 indicators","url":"https://data.unicef.org/country/egy/","claim":"UNICEF's Egypt country dashboard reports 2022 safely managed drinking-water access at 79 percent and safely managed sanitation access at 57 percent.","locator":"WASH sections, lines 5627-5676","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF country dashboard is a primary indicator source; the values are directly visible on the page.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"2022 indicator snapshot","ruler_attribution":"Country-level outcome context only; not a direct ruler-action measure.","contrary_evidence":["The dashboard is outcome data, so it cannot isolate Sisi's personal contribution.","Significant gaps remain, especially in sanitation."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Child-Related SDG Progress Assessment for Egypt","publisher":"UNICEF DATA","publication_date":"best available; page shows 2022/2020/2017 values","url":"https://data.unicef.org/sdgs/country/egy/","claim":"UNICEF's child-SDG dashboard for Egypt reports 3 percent of children in extreme poverty in 2022, 33 percent of children below the national poverty line in 2017, and 14 percent child-population coverage by social protection floors/systems in 2020.","locator":"poverty section, lines 5050-5056 and 5063-5063","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"UNICEF country dashboard is a primary indicator source with direct visible values and methodology context.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"mixed-period context surrounding 2022","ruler_attribution":"Country-level outcome context only; useful mainly for identifying residual deprivation and limited coverage, not direct personal action.","contrary_evidence":["The page mixes different reference years and cannot by itself measure 2022 ruler performance.","It does not separate national policy effects from inherited trends."],"lenses":["6B.3","6B.8","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"WHO Egypt and Ministry of Health and Population celebrate physicians on Family Doctor Day","publisher":"WHO Eastern Mediterranean Regional Office","publication_date":"2022-05-25","url":"https://www.emro.who.int/egy/egypt-news/who-egypt-and-ministry-of-health-celebrate-physicians-on-family-doctor-day.html","claim":"WHO and Egypt's Ministry of Health held a 2022 conference for 200 primary health-care workers and said family physicians are the first point of contact for essential care and health emergencies, while WHO continued technical and capacity-building support for family medicine.","locator":"lines 228-234","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous WHO country-office report on a concrete 2022 event and support activity.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"exactly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Ministry of Health under the Sisi government, though the source also reflects WHO and USAID support.","contrary_evidence":["A conference and training support do not prove system-wide service quality.","The source emphasizes staffing shortages and the need for further investment."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"WHO Egypt works towards improving the hospital sector in Egypt","publisher":"WHO Eastern Mediterranean Regional Office","publication_date":"2022-09-06","url":"https://www.emro.who.int/egy/egypt-news/who-egypt-works-towards-improving-the-hospital-sector-in-egypt.html","claim":"WHO said it worked with Egyptian health authorities in August 2022 to develop the national strategic plan for the hospital sector, and that Egypt was taking active steps to implement universal health insurance and build a more resilient health system.","locator":"lines 228-233","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Contemporaneous WHO country-office report describing a specific policy workshop and the government's UHI implementation direction.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"exactly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the Sisi-era health ministry and linked institutions, though implementation was collaborative with WHO and higher-education bodies.","contrary_evidence":["The source is a workshop report, not an outcome evaluation.","It notes system-development challenges rather than completed reform."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.5","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Health systems strengthening towards universal health coverage","publisher":"WHO Eastern Mediterranean Regional Office","publication_date":"best available","url":"https://www.emro.who.int/egy/programmes/health-systems-strengthening.html","claim":"WHO said it actively supported Egypt's Ministry of Health in pursuing universal health coverage and described Egypt's health system as fragmented, with unequal access and large out-of-pocket burdens, while arguing that an equity-driven health sector can help break the illness-poverty cycle.","locator":"programme overview, lines 225-239","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"medium_high","source_confidence_reason":"WHO country programme page is a strong institutional source, but it is general background rather than a 2022 event report.","final_evidence_use":"context","period_fit":"baseline and ongoing context around 2022","ruler_attribution":"Country-system context rather than a discrete Sisi action, though it addresses the system his government governed.","contrary_evidence":["The page is framed as long-run support and baseline context, not a year-specific performance audit.","It does not measure 2022 delivery."],"lenses":["6B.4","6B.6","6B.10"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Improving Wastewater Services Program - Upper Egypt","publisher":"Egypt Ministry of International Cooperation","publication_date":"2022-02-10","url":"https://moic.gov.eg/project/149","claim":"Egypt signed a CHF 7.2 million grant on 10 February 2022 for an Aswan water project under SDG 6, with related goals of good health and reduced inequalities.","locator":"project summary lines 66-114","source_type":"official","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Official ministry project page with a dated financing agreement and explicit location/objective information.","final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"exactly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributed to the Sisi government through its international-cooperation ministry, though the project was donor-financed and locally implemented.","contrary_evidence":["The page gives project metadata rather than completion or impact results.","It is a grant announcement, not an implementation audit."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.9"]}
SOURCE_CLAIM_JSON: {"title":"Sustainable Rural Sanitation Services Program for Egypt","publisher":"World Bank","publication_date":"2022-09-12","url":"https://www.worldbank.org/en/news/infographic/2022/09/12/sustainable-rural-sanitation-services-program-for-egypt","claim":"The World Bank said its rural sanitation program for Egypt aimed to increase access to and services of rural sanitation in Beheira, Dakahliya, and Sharkiya, improving citizens' well-being and protecting agricultural soil.","locator":"infographic text, lines 14-35","source_type":"IGO","source_confidence":"high","source_confidence_reason":"Dated World Bank project infographic with explicit geography and objective." ,"final_evidence_use":"final_evidence","period_fit":"exactly within 2022","ruler_attribution":"Attributable to the broader Sisi-era state program and its development partners, not to a single ministerial decision.","contrary_evidence":["The infographic is a goal statement, not proof of finished service expansion in 2022.","It does not quantify completed households or outcomes."],"lenses":["6B.2","6B.3","6B.9"]}

**Search and rejection summary**
I considered roughly 30 documents across the World Bank, IMF, WHO, UNICEF, WFP, Egypt's Ministry of International Cooperation, and some later retrospectives.
- Kept the 11 URLs above because they were Egypt-specific and either dated in 2022 or directly retrospective on the target period.
- Dropped repeated/global sources such as WHO's generic WASH annual report, WHO primary-health-care explainers, and UNICEF global education reports because they were useful background but not Egypt-specific ruler-period evidence.
- Dropped later 2024-2026 country pages and global dashboards when they did not add target-period specificity beyond the 2022 indicator snapshot.
- Dropped older background sources, including the 2018 rural sanitation press release, when the 2022 project pages or 2022 country dashboards carried the same claim with better period fit.
- Dropped broad macro or program-summary pages that repeated the same social-protection story without adding distinct evidence, especially where the World Bank 2022 press releases already captured the key numbers.
- Dropped a few inaccessible or low-value pages that did not produce usable Egypt-specific lines, including generic landing pages and document shells.

**Remaining gaps**
- The clearest gap is direct ruler-level attribution for social welfare decisions. The dossier shows Sisi-era government action, but most sources are ministry, WHO, UNICEF, or World Bank mediated rather than a direct presidential welfare statement.
- I still have only partial evidence on implementation quality, especially whether 2022 programs actually reduced learning loss, poverty, or malnutrition by year-end rather than simply expanding coverage.
- I have good coverage on food security, cash transfers, health-system reform, education remediation, and WASH, but weaker direct evidence on housing, disability inclusion, and durable post-2022 institutional performance.
- More search would help on household survey data, program evaluations, and any 2022 Egyptian budget or cabinet material that links these interventions more directly to el-Sisi's personal agenda and oversight.

No score assigned.
